    "reading": {
        "body": "Maryland's mapped data center footprint across this dataset covers 11 facilities, concentrated heavily in and around Baltimore, with no power capacity figures recorded for any of them.\n\nThe geographic clustering is notable. Eight of the eleven facilities sit within or immediately adjacent to Baltimore city, with the remaining three in Prince George's County, Anne Arundel County, and Owings Mills. That proximity suggests shared reliance on regional fiber routes and utility infrastructure rather than deliberate geographic distribution for redundancy. The operators range from national carriers like Lumen Technologies and Crown Castle Fiber to regional specialists like Expedient and AiNET, which operates at least three of the listed sites.\n\nThe absence of megawatt figures is the most significant limitation of this data. Facility count alone says nothing about the power draw each site places on the grid, the capacity available for expansion, or whether any of these locations could support the dense compute loads that large-scale AI workloads require. A single hyperscale campus can exceed the combined draw of all eleven sites listed here, or fall well short of it. Without power data, the connection between this map and AI's binding constraint on grid capacity cannot be drawn from what is recorded.",
